id: pm-urzubi
title: "Starting work ignores open blockers: claim <id> --start and update --status in_progress move a blocked item into progress without a refusal or warning"
description: "Raised by CodeRabbit on PR #1645 and verified on 2026.10.31 source in a fresh sandbox. An item created with --blocked-by <open item> is stored as status blocked, and pm update <id> --status in_progress then succeeds with no refusal or warning. pm claim <id> --start runs the same claim-then-update composition, so it behaves the same. pm validate later reports non_blocked_status_has_blocked_by for the in-progress item whose blocker is still open. pm next and claim --next --start are unaffected because ready selection already excludes items with open blockers. Starting an explicitly blocked item is a governance decision, not a claim defect, so changing only --start would make start and update disagree.\n\nDuplicate search: 'start blocked item open blocker in_progress refuse', 'non_blocked_status_has_blocked_by' — no open item. Nearest is the dependency-aware actionability decision (closed)."
type: Issue
status: closed
priority: 3
tags[4]: agent-ux,dependencies,governance,lifecycle
created_at: "2026-10-31T23:01:47.892Z"
updated_at: "2026-11-03T01:13:52.332Z"
closed_at: "2026-11-02T14:26:36.511Z"
completed_at: "2026-11-02T14:26:36.511Z"
author: "harness:claude-code"
estimated_minutes: 150
acceptance_criteria: "Moving an item with an open blocked_by target into the in-progress status, through update, claim --start or start-task, either refuses with a typed code naming the open blocker and the unblock or --force recovery, or warns, as governed by a declared setting; the CLI, MCP and SDK paths share one check that runs under the lock of each write that would start the item, so the explicit claim --start judges it before writing ownership and again before its status update; a refusal at the second check releases a claim the start took from no owner and otherwise discloses the partial completion; a terminal blocker does not block; tests cover refuse, warn, terminal-blocker and blocker-opened-between-writes cases"
parent: pm-33cw
risk: medium
confidence: medium
resolution: "sdk/lifecycle/start-blockers.ts owns one admission check, resolved through sdk/query/declared-blockers.ts (shared with the pm get blockers facet) and governed by governance.start_blocker_enforcement (strict default, warn, off). update --status, claim --next --start and start-task run it under the item lock of each write that would start the item. The explicit claim --start judges it before writing ownership and again at its status update. When a blocker opens between the two writes, the start releases a claim it took from no owner (fenced against a peer takeover); otherwise the refusal's why reports the partial completion. Recovery steps are shell-quoted and offered only where a single command is safe."
expected_result: No transport can silently move an item with an open blocker into progress; the behaviour is declared by governance settings and consistent across update and start.
actual_result: "Verified in specs and against the built CLI. update/claim --start/start-task refuse with exit 4 when the claim-time check fails, writing nothing. A deterministic race spec shows a blocker opened between claim and update refuses, releases the claim from no owner (history: create, claim, update, release), keeps an earlier owner's claim with a partial-completion why, and never releases a takeover by another principal (a concurrent release-and-reclaim by the same principal is indistinguishable, which is documented). Same-call unblock+start, terminal blockers and already-in-progress items are admitted; --force and warn record their warnings. The published 2026.11.2 negative control starts the blocked item silently."
