comments[6]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-11-02T13:29:12.559Z","harness:claude-code","Implementation evidence (feat/write-time-preconditions): sdk/lifecycle/start-blockers.ts owns one admission check, admitStartWithOpenBlockers, governed by the new governance.start_blocker_enforcement (strict default, warn, off; pm config key governance-start-blocker-enforcement). It resolves blockers through sdk/query/declared-blockers.ts, the resolution pm get's blockers facet already used, extracted so the two cannot disagree. Terminal targets resolve; missing, unsafe and external references stay open. Hooks: runUpdate checks inside its locked mutate callback on the UPDATED metadata, so an update that removes the edge and starts in one call is admitted. runClaim(start) checks under the claim lock (claim --next --start). runStartTask pre-checks before the claim write, so a refused claim <id> --start leaves no claim, and the update re-checks under its own lock. Refusal start_blocked_by_open_dependency (exit 4) lists details.open_blockers and an executable unblock command (--unset blocked_by and/or --dep-remove id=<b>,kind=blocked_by, chosen from where the blocker is declared). Verified the advertised command then lets the start succeed. --force admits with start_blocker_override:<id>:<blockers>, and warn mode records start_with_open_blockers:<id>:<blockers>."
  "2026-11-02T15:18:39.843Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 1 on PR 1654: the unlocked pre-check in runStartTask let a blocker added between the pre-check and the update leave a claim behind. Fixed: runAsExplicitStart makes the claim judge the start under its own item lock before writing ownership (admitExplicitStartClaim), and the composition's status update reuses that decision instead of re-judging. claim --next --start keeps its single locked write."
  "2026-11-02T17:47:13.105Z","harness:claude-code","Review round 2+3 on PR 1654. The composed status update always re-judges under its own lock. When a blocker opens after the claim, the start releases the claim it took from no owner, and keeps the original refusal if that release fails. A deterministic race spec (start-race.spec.ts) covers it. Suggested commands render through renderPmCommand (CodeRabbit Major: stored blocker ids are now shell-quoted). Blocker kinds canonicalize (depends_on resolves instead of throwing), short aliases compare canonically and edges are cited as stored. --unset blocked_by is advertised only when no other blocked_by edge would be lost; that data-loss defect is filed as pm-4yxzvd."
  "2026-11-02T19:58:19.090Z","harness:claude-code","Review round 4 on PR 1654. The claim compensation is fenced under the item lock, so it never releases a peer's takeover (under warn ownership enforcement a plain release would have). Start detection uses the effective start status (resolveStartTaskInProgressStatus), including the registered in_progress fallback when the workflow names open. Warning dedupe now drops only the exact warnings the claim recorded, so a blocker found only at the status write still warns. --dep-remove is advertised only for literal blocked_by rows whose stored id survives selector normalization; legacy short-id, alias-kind and external rows get an inspection step (gap recorded on pm-4yxzvd). The refusal message no longer claims 'Nothing was written'. The acceptance criteria, resolution and actual result were corrected to the two-check composition semantics."
  "2026-11-02T20:42:54.485Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 4 on PR 1654: the start-compensation fence compares the locked claim_principal as well as the display assignee, so a takeover by another session with the same assignee is never released. Spec added in start-race.spec.ts."
  "2026-11-02T23:09:39.363Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 9 on PR 1654. The start-compensation fence compares assignee and claim_principal. A concurrent release-and-reclaim by the SAME principal between the refused status write and the compensation is indistinguishable from the original claim, because principal is pm's unit of ownership and claims carry no generation token. That case is documented in docs/COMMANDS.md rather than fenced. A claim-generation token belongs with the fenced-claim-generation work in pm-gh1359. Also fixed: start_blocked_by_open_dependency is declared as a literal, so the error-code catalog lists it (emitters claim, start-task, update)."
